{"authored" [{"question" "q-16380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$-10{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+20x+36y=64?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013817/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a685444faccd327a9a553c528ef51d6a23e914a34faf9f99d0a9afa2864db2d" "basis" 1173 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013817/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-16380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+20x+36y & =64 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+36y-10{x}^{2}+20x & =64 \\\\ 9[{y}^{2}+4y]-10[{x}^{2}-2x] & =64 \\\\ 9[(y+2)^{2}-4]-10[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =64 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-36-10(x-1)^{2}+10 & =64 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-10(x-1)^{2} & =64+36-10 \\\\ 9(y+2)^{2}-10(x-1)^{2} & =90 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{10}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(1,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013817/state.json" "file_sha256" "0a685444faccd327a9a553c528ef51d6a23e914a34faf9f99d0a9afa2864db2d" "basis" 1173 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013817/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-16380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013817/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "08283654b18d4f3db96e73000f824a9f15e18318a753ff27764d0667804ac18d" "basis" 1173 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_A